 ## Overview
-This method computes the gradient of the rendered image with respect to scene parameters $\theta$ by integrating differential measurement contributions over path space [S1]. It solves the problem of high variance in gradient estimation for scenes with varying material properties (e.g., roughness) by combining standard BRDF sampling with Next Event Estimation (NEE) using Multiple Importance Sampling (MIS) [S1]. Unlike standard NEE which only connects paths to emitters at the final vertex, this method allows the differential vertex to be located at any intermediate vertex or at the emitter connection, effectively sharing paths to calculate derivatives with respect to all scene parameters simultaneously [S1][S2, unofficial].
+This method computes the gradient of the rendered image with respect to scene parameters $\theta$ by integrating differential measurement contributions over path space, using Multiple Importance Sampling (MIS) to combine BRDF and emitter sampling, and Next Event Estimation (NEE) to connect paths to emitters [S1]. It solves the problem of high variance in gradient estimation for scenes with varying material properties by allowing the "differential vertex" (the vertex where the parameter derivative is applied) to be located at any intermediate vertex or at the emitter connection, rather than being restricted to specific locations [S1]. Unlike standard NEE which only connects paths to emitters at the final vertex, this method combines BRDF and emitter sampling probabilities into a single mixture weight, significantly reducing variance for microfacet and mixture BRDFs [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-The estimator constructs an unbiased Monte Carlo estimate of the gradient $\nabla_\theta I$ by sampling two types of paths: standard paths generated via BRDF sampling, and side paths generated via NEE that connect an intermediate vertex to an emitter. The contributions are weighted by a mixture probability that accounts for the likelihood of generating each path configuration under both BRDF and emitter sampling strategies [S1].
+The estimator computes the gradient $\partial_\theta I$ of the rendering integral $I$ by sampling paths and weighting their differential contributions by the inverse of a mixture probability that accounts for all ways the path could have been generated (via BRDF sampling or NEE) [S1].
 
